        "content": [
          "Italian pairs pronouns constantly.",
          "When a DIRECT OBJECT meets an INDIRECT",
          "OBJECT, they fuse into tight clusters.",
          "",
          "THE TRANSFORMATION RULE:",
          "   mi, ti, ci, vi, si → me, te, ce, ve, se",
          "   (before lo, la, li, le, ne)",
          "",
          "EXAMPLES:",
          "   Mi dà il libro.  → Me lo dà.",
          "   Ti mando la foto → Te la mando.",
          "   Ci porta i fiori → Ce li porta.",
          "   Vi spedisco le   → Ve le spedisco.",
          "      cartoline.",
          "   Si lava le mani  → Se le lava.",
          "   Se ne va.        = He's leaving.",
          "",
          "The IO goes FIRST, the DO second."
        ],
        "pronunciation": "Me lo dà."
      },
      {
        "page": 2,
        "total_pages": 3,
        "content": [
          "GLI (him) and LE (her) behave differently.",
          "They merge with a DO into ONE FIXED WORD.",
          "",
          "THE GLIE-FAMILY:",
          "",
          "   gli/le + lo  →  glielo",
          "   gli/le + la  →  gliela",
          "   gli/le + li  →  glieli",
          "   gli/le + le  →  gliele",
          "   gli/le + ne  →  gliene",
          "",
          "Same form serves 'to him' AND 'to her'.",
          "",
          "EXAMPLES:",
          "",
          "   Gli do il libro    → Glielo do.",
          "   Le dico la verità  → Gliela dico.",
          "   Gli presto i soldi → Glieli presto."
        ]
      },
      {
        "page": 3,
        "total_pages": 3,
        "content": [
          "POSITION — where these clusters go:",
          "",
          "BEFORE conjugated verbs:",
          "",
          "   Me lo dà.     = He gives it to me.",
          "   Glielo mando. = I send it to him.",
          "   Gliene parlo. = I tell him of it.",
          "",
          "ATTACHED to infinitives, imperatives,",
          "and gerunds — written as ONE word:",
          "",
          "   voglio dartelo  = to give it to you",
          "   dammelo!        = give it to me!",
          "   diglielo!       = tell him!",
          "   parlandogliene  = telling him of it",
          "",
          "Short tu-imperatives double the consonant",
          "(dammi, dimmi), but never gli: diglielo."
        ]
      }
